<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meenakshi Temple</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meenakshi Amman Temple</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meenakshi Temple (Meenakshi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> Temple) is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temple complex in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, dedicated to the goddess Meenakshi and her consort Sundareswarar, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Its present buildings, with fourteen towering painted gopurams, were largely rebuilt by the Madurai <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayaks</a> in the 16th and 17th centuries, notably under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirumala-nayak">Tirumala Nayak</a>. It remains one of the most visited temples in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meenakshi-temple.jpg" alt="Meenakshi Temple" width="320" height="105" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: எஸ்ஸார் · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_aerial_view_of_Madurai_city_from_atop_of_Meenakshi_Amman_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Tamil texts imply that a temple existed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> by the mid-6th century, and the town of Koodal, as Madurai was called, is described as a temple town from which every street radiated. The temple is one of the Paadal Petra Sthalams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> temples praised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tevaram">Tevaram</a> hymns composed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> between the 6th and 11th centuries. Most surviving inscriptions are dated after the 12th century, so the early structures are known chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> period of the 12th to 13th centuries.</p><p>After the death of Maravarman Kulasekhara I in 1310, his sons fought over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> throne while the Khalji dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> raided the south. Records say that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> looted and destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples, the Meenakshi temple among them, and carried gold and jewellery to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1311. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai">Madurai</a> in 1378, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana">Kumara Kampana</a> is said to have rebuilt the temple, fortified it with walls and restored the hidden idols.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The temple stands on the southern bank of the Vaigai River and is dedicated to Meenakshi, a form of Parvati, and her consort Sundareswarar, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. Meenakshi means fish-eyed in Tamil. According to the Thiruvilaiyadal Puranam, she appeared from a sacrificial fire as the daughter of King Malayadhwaja Pandya, ruled as his successor and took her true form when she met Shiva. Her shrine stands at the centre of the inner enclosure, to the right of Sundareswarar's, and pilgrims visit it first.</p><p>The complex covers 5.7 hectares inside concentric walled enclosures. It has 14 gopurams, the towers over its gateways, of which the tallest, the southern tower built in the 16th century, rises to 52 metres. The thousand-pillared hall holds 985 carved pillars, and the sacred tank is called the Golden Lotus Pond. The streets around the temple are named after months of the Tamil calendar, because processions began from the spots matching each month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The temple has long been a centre of Tamil literature. The poet Kumaraguruparar wrote the Meenakshi Pillaitamil in praise of the goddess in the 17th century, and Shyama Shastri composed nine Telugu songs for her, the Navaratnamalika. Its yearly wedding festival, the Meenakshi thirukalyanam, lasts 15 days in the month of Chithirai and is the most attended of the temple's monthly festivals.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalit">Dalits</a> were barred from entering the temple until the early 20th century. On 8 July 1939 a group led by A. Vaidyanatha Iyer and Kakkan entered it, an event that preceded legislation of the Madras Presidency later that year. The Hindu Religious and Charitable Endowments Department of the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> manages the temple, and the state's emblem is based on its west gopuram.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meenakshi Temple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tirumala Nayak, in the 17th century, rebuilt much of the <b>Meenakshi Amman temple</b> and built the Tirumalai Nayak palace in Madurai.</blockquote><a href="/state/madurai-nayak-dynasty">Madurai Nayak dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
